[ceiling] This is the 13 by 14.15 by 1.60 mm touch flag over the 6 mm wide eye: approximately 3.50 mm projection per X side and 3.85 mm forward projection. It is an accessible handle/finish feature, separate from the 0.24/0.48/0.72 upper-release planes. Existing native first flag layers use Bridge and Overhang-wall deposits with supports off; these small cantilevers remain an unprinted finish/handling risk, not a missing-path finding.
alternatives: A roughly 3.85 mm taller tapered shoulder can grow to the same handle width above the existing eye roof while preserving the release planes, but increases shutter height and needs a new travel/neighbor-clearance review. A narrower flag removes the side projection but gives less touch/pull area. A separately attached larger handle adds a joint and another part to the captive assembly.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/q24-captive-ripple-shutter.step
click: x=-49.000 y=62.075 z=9.080

[ceiling] This is the captive eye's required solid-skin upper release ceiling, with 2.40 mm roof stock. Leaf-only lint measures the drop to its own 2.60 mm floor; the four-body manufacturing assembly places the fixed rail top at Z 6.44, giving CAD roof air 0.24/0.48/0.72 mm for Q24/Q48/Q72. Native roof nozzle Z 6.92/7.16/7.40 minus the 0.24 mm model step gives the same nominal underside/air. No model/support deposits occupy the release windows; physical strand sag, contact and release remain unmeasured. The solid skins are a distinct comparison from sparse support interfaces.
alternatives: A sloped or open roof avoids this ceiling but removes capture or changes the flat upper-contact test. Raising the roof to a larger gap is a different comparison rung, not a correction inferred from this leaf-only drop. A separately assembled eye can use thicker open sections while giving up the print-in-place release question.
